  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1215 AM Sunday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Rain chances and amounts are still highly uncertain \nlate this week. Below normal temperatures and the potential for \nwidespread frost/freeze is expected next weekend.\n\nAfter a chilly start this morning under cool high pressure, \ntemperatures should climb into the 60s this afternoon as return flow \nsets in and high clouds move east. A warming trend with temperatures \nsome 5-10 degrees above average will take shape this upcoming week \nas low-level thicknesses rise and periods of return southwest flow \nremain in place. Some patchy frost could occur Tuesday morning under \na night of clear skies and light winds. A mid-level disturbance \ncrosses the region Wednesday, which could increase clouds but offer \nlimited rain chances as Gulf moisture is essentially closed off.\n\nWe continue to watch a late-week storm system that is poised to \ndevelop as an Omega block sets up with ridging across the western US \nand troughing in the eastern US. A dip in the polar jet stream \noccurs across the east, phasing with the subtropical jet, leading to \ndownstream cyclogenesis. The aggregate of ensemble members, \nincluding AI guidance, shows a surface low track across the northern \nMid-Atlantic states Thursday into Friday. However, there are still \nsome outlier solutions which track the low further south across the \nTN valley or even over the Carolinas. The further south solutions \nwould offer a better chance of accumulated rainfall along the \ndeformation band, while the northern consensus trend would bring \nisolated rain amounts. The best chance with this scenario would be \nalong the cold front Thursday afternoon or evening, which if it \ntrends far enough north could bring a threat of storms with the area \npossibly in the warm sector.\n\nThere appears more widespread agreement amongst the guidance that as \nthe system passes, a much colder airmass settles in with thicknesses \naround 1300m, supporting widespread frost/freeze next weekend. The \nonly limiting factor may be wind gusts, which could be elevated with \na moderate pressure gradient in place.",
